preConditionHook:
// THE MARGINAL BREAKOUT THAT FAILED. The PRIOR bar poked at most barelyTicks
// ticks beyond the extreme of the bars BEFORE it, and THIS bar has closed back
// on the other side of that level -- Brooks's "breaks a resistance level by
// only a tick or so and then reverses". The trade is the reversal, so the LONG
// side is a failed DOWNSIDE breakout and the short side a failed upside one.
// The merged pseudocode spelled the upside poke, which is the SELL setup; the
// pair is mirrored, so the long side is written first here.
//
// THE LEVEL IS Highest(High, lookback)[2] -- the extreme of the lookback bars
// ENDING TWO BARS AGO, i.e. the bars BEFORE the poking bar, so the poke cannot
// define the level it pokes. That two-bar offset is the part of this rule that
// is easy to get wrong and the reason the merge graded it B.
//
// R02-27's second, higher level is DROPPED: it needs a remembered set of
// resistance levels, and on a fixed-window level "did not reach the next level"
// is implied by having reversed back under the first.
//
// NOT SmallBreakoutTrapReversal, which is the ONE-BAR form -- the same bar
// pokes through and closes back -- with the cap in PRICE POINTS; this needs the
// poke and the recovery on DIFFERENT bars, so the two never fire together. Not
// FailedBreakoutReversal, whose poke bar must CLOSE beyond the level (this one
// need only trade beyond it with its high) and which carries no cap at all. Not
// ThirdBarConfirmsFailedBreakout, a two-bar body-comparison pattern with no
// level in it.
//
// The window scan mirrors EasyLanguage's Extremes(), which Highest() and
// Lowest() call: seeded at the window's first bar, scanning the rest, strict
// comparison, EL SOURCE and so carrying the relational tolerance -- el_gt /
// el_lt, not raw > and <.
// THE TICK is EL's own MinMove / PriceScale, both VERIFIED and per-symbol
// constants for the whole run, written DIVISION-FIRST in both twins so the C++
// reads token for token against its EasyLanguage. Read once into a local.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the generator
// only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a redefinition
// when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. Every one is fully assigned
// each bar before it is read.
tickSz = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
hiLevel = high[2];
loLevel = low[2];
for (int i = 3; i <= (int)lookback + 1; ++i) {
    if (el_gt(high[i], hiLevel)) hiLevel = high[i];
    if (el_lt(low[i], loLevel)) loLevel = low[i];
}
